# README Documentation - Project Structure

This rule provides guidelines for creating effective README.md files and maintaining project documentation structure that serves both human users and automated systems.

## 1. File Naming Conventions and Navigation

### 1.1. Core Documentation Files

- **README.md**: Project overview, quick start, essential information (optimized for first impression)
- **CONTEXT.md**: Detailed background, business context, system rationale (comprehensive AI context)
- **API.md**: API documentation with structured endpoints and examples
- **ARCHITECTURE.md**: System design, component relationships, technical decisions
- **DEPLOYMENT.md**: Infrastructure setup, deployment procedures, operational guides
- **TROUBLESHOOTING.md**: Problem-solution pairs, debugging workflows, incident procedures
- **docs/**: Directory for comprehensive documentation collections

### 1.2. README.md Navigation Structure

Always include a **Documentation** section in README.md that links to available documentation files:

```markdown
# Project Name

## Overview
[Brief project description optimized for context building]

## Quick Start
[Essential setup steps with validation checkpoints]

## Documentation

### Core Documentation
- 📋 [Project Context](CONTEXT.md) - Detailed background and system rationale
- 🏗️ [Architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md) - System design and component relationships
- 🚀 [Deployment](DEPLOYMENT.md) - Infrastructure setup and operational procedures
- 🔧 [API Reference](API.md) - Endpoints, parameters, and examples
- 🔍 [Troubleshooting](TROUBLESHOOTING.md) - Common issues and solutions

### Extended Documentation
- 📚 [User Guide](docs/user-guide.md) - End-user instructions and workflows
- 👩‍💻 [Developer Guide](docs/developer-guide.md) - Development setup and contribution guidelines
- 🔐 [Security Guide](docs/security.md) - Security considerations and best practices

## Contributing
[Contribution guidelines with clear next steps]
```

